---

<Base
  title="About — Sarah Barns"
  banner={{
    src: 'https://pub-9840de99aef84197989ef35cf41fa967.r2.dev/docklands/docklands-water.mp4',
    captionTitle: 'Docklands Waterscape',
    captionBody: 'Wurundjuri lands, Naarm/Melbourne.',
  }}
>
  <div class="page-header about-header">
    <p class="about-kicker"><span class="about-tick"></span><em>the longer story</em></p>
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="page-subtitle">I create, cultivate and reflect on digital interfaces and experiences as they shape our relationship to places in transition. I write, I listen, I collaborate, I imagine.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose practice">
    <p class="practice-lead">My work speaks to the transformation of cities and places through digital
      technologies. While many urban technologists see the city as data, I see it as an archive of
      noisy events, voices, habitats and governing conditions. I deliberately straddle scholarship and
      collaborative practice, lean heavily into live experimentation as strategy, and am committed to
      ensuring the public record is one that future generations can continue to be inspired by and
      shape with agency and purpose.</p>

    <p>Practice-wise I work with teams to build and prototype helpful interfaces and story-lines that
      connect us to the places we live in and that sustain us. This work is funded through a range of
      partnerships and collaborations: research fellowships, start-up grants, project commissions,
      and self-funded and government R&amp;D.</p>

    <p>My key platforms are <a href="https://storybox.co">STORYBOX Places</a>,
      <a href="https://civicinterplay.io">Civic Interplay</a> and
      <a href="https://studioesem.com">Studio ESEM</a>. My current research enquiry is the collisions
      of AI sovereignties and citizen-AI activism in cities, and the governance protocols and pathways
      that underpin them. My disciplinary home is urban digital geography, specifically the historical
      and political geography of digital cities.</p>

    <p class="practice-intent">Primary intention: creating spaces and places of care, creativity and
      everyday enlivenment.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose longer">
    <h2 class="about-h2 longer-head">The longer story</h2>

    <p>I came to this through sound, and I have never really left. My PhD used ambient sound archives
      to build mobile listening experiences that surfaced contested spatial histories, reimagining
      real-time city technologies as connection points to the real times of space. In 2008 that became
      <em>Sydney Sidetracks</em>, the ABC's first location-based service, which let people stand in
      Martin Place and hear it in 1945. Everything since has carried the same method: soundscapes for
      Kings Cross and Kate Leigh, essays on sirens and sensors, an immersive ecological work under a
      tree canopy, interviews recorded on the water with people fighting for a river. Listening is not
      a phase of the work. It is how I do the work.</p>

    <p>In 2012 I negotiated the release of ABC television and radio archive material under Creative
      Commons, through a partnership with ABC Pool called Past Forward. Talbot Duckmanton reporting
      the end of the war, Juanita Nielsen on why she loved the Cross the year before she disappeared,
      Bea Miles, Chow Hayes, Wendy Bacon marking an eviction on Victoria Street. Those recordings are
      publicly listenable because that agreement exists. It changed what was possible for other people
      rather than only for me.</p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Strategy and advisory</h2>

    <p>Alongside this I have been a strategic advisor for over twenty years. I wrote the ABC's
      organisation-wide digital strategy and the Australia Council's first national creative industries
      strategy, and have delivered research and strategy for the Department of the Prime Minister and
      Cabinet through Data61, UCL's Institute for Innovation and Public Purpose, Create NSW, the NSW
      Government Architect and the Australian Museum. This is not separate from the research. Working
      inside institutions is how you learn what actually governs them.</p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Studio ESEM and STORYBOX Places</h2>

    <p>I co-founded Esem Projects in 2013, now <a href="https://studioesem.com">Studio ESEM</a>,
      working with cultural institutions, councils and communities to build participatory digital
      infrastructure. Its signature platform is <a href="https://storybox.co">STORYBOX Places</a>,
      which since 2020 has put outdoor exhibition infrastructure into public space for community
      voice, with the ABC, the Australian Museum, the National Gallery of Australia, UNSW and
      Canterbury Bankstown Council, building on more than forty placemaking programs. I also publish
      <a href="https://civicinterplay.io">Civic Interplay</a>.</p>

    <p>My current enquiry is the collision of AI sovereignties with citizen-AI activism in cities, and
      the governance protocols that underpin them. It follows from <em>Platform Urbanism</em>
      (Palgrave Macmillan, 2019), which asked what alternative platform models look like when designed
      for public value rather than private extraction, and has since been cited over 700 times.</p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Golden threads</h2>

    <p>Running through the scholarship, the studio practice and the writing is what I think of as a
      long-form narrative inquiry into how digital systems reshape place, citizenship and collective
      governance. Golden threads structuring space through time, resisting what I call the actuarial
      colonisation of the unknown. I am writing a book about it, <em>Still Here</em>, structured as a
      topographical excavation of sites through modes of encounter: listening, reading, installing,
      diagnosing, gathering, letting go.</p>

    <p>I am based in Sydney. Beyond the research I keep a body-based practice through Pilates, yoga,
      cycling and dance, and a long-standing interest in deep-time writing as a counterweight to the
      accelerationist logics of the systems I study.</p>
  </div>

